review_comments:
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/107854#discussion_r796727758

The whole block solves the problem that we could not remove a pod worker of the static pod that has been terminated before start.

We have to clean up the undesired pod worker that does not start yet or there is another static pod with the same name.

And we have to check waiting here because there is no way to check whether it is a static pod.

What I wanted to do is that

```go
if staticPod {
    if !started || (started && startedUID != uid) {
        klog.V(4).InfoS("Pod does not start yet but is no longer known to the kubelet, finish it", "podUID", uid)
	status.finished = true
    }
}

> I'm asking "why do you need to process this here" - i.e. describe the problem this code block is trying to solve.

@smarterclayton 

This is the case that I want to cover in this PR. If a static pod is updated multiple times during grace termination seconds, not started and terminated static pods can pile up to `waitingToStartStaticPodsByFullname`. And if the old static pod is terminated finally, there is no started static pod for the name. And `removeTerminatedWorker` would not remove not started and terminated static pods because there is no started static pod for the name(which is the previous condition).
